FDM patch 0003: switch the body-rate scheme while a contact point touches the ground
(candidate 3 of session 04)

What it changes
  JSBSim integrates the body rates with one scheme, chosen by simulation/integrator/rate/rotational.
  The multistep schemes (AB2 to AB4) are accurate in flight and fragile on the ground; Euler is the
  reverse (REPORT-03). With this patch a second scheme can take over while the aircraft touches the
  ground:
    simulation/integrator/rate/rotational-on-contact   integer, default -1
       -1  no switching: JSBSim unchanged.
       an integrator id (1 = Euler): that scheme integrates the body rates in every step whose
       derivative was computed with a contact point touching, and for contact-hold-sec afterwards.
       In all other steps simulation/integrator/rate/rotational is used, as before.
    simulation/integrator/rate/contact-hold-sec        seconds, default 0
       how long the on-contact scheme stays after the last contact (against rapid switching while
       bouncing or chattering).
    simulation/integrator/rate/contact-release-mode    integer, default 1
       what happens to the multistep history when the in-air scheme takes over again:
       1  the history of past derivatives is restarted from the current derivative, exactly as JSBSim
          does at start-up. The first step in the air is then an Euler step with the current
          derivative: no extra impulse.
       0  the history is kept. It still holds derivatives that contain ground forces, and the
          extrapolation adds an impulse of about half a step of the last ground moment. Kept for
          comparison only.
    simulation/integrator/rate/contact-active, .../contact-switches
       which scheme the latest step used, and how often the scheme has changed (counters; the
       simulation never reads them).

How contact is detected
  FGFDMExec::LoadInputs passes "does any contact point touch" to FGPropagate before each step: every
  point of FGGroundReactions counts, wheels (BOGEY) and airframe (STRUCTURE), each by its own WOW flag.
  gear/wow is not used, because it ignores the airframe points. The flag belongs to the same instant
  as the derivative that the step integrates, so a derivative that contains ground forces is always
  integrated by the on-contact scheme.

Why nothing else is needed on touch-down
  The on-contact scheme used here (Euler) needs no history, so switching to it adds nothing. The
  history deque keeps being filled, as in unpatched JSBSim.

Interaction with patch 0001 (integrator-aware friction)
  Friction forces exist only while a contact point touches, and then the on-contact scheme integrates
  them. So the accessor that tells the friction code which body-rate scheme is in use returns the
  on-contact scheme when the switch is on.

How it is switched off
  With rotational-on-contact = -1 (the default) the step uses integrator_rotational_rate through the
  same calls as before. Checked: bit-identical logs (tests/native/verify_key.sh patched unpatched,
  tests/native/verify_wasm_patch_off.sh). FGPropagate::Inputs gains one flag, which is only read when
  the switch is on. Like JSBSim's own integrator selections, the new settings return to their defaults
  when the model is re-initialised.

diff --git a/src/FGFDMExec.cpp b/src/FGFDMExec.cpp
index 363c187..45cba81 100644
--- a/src/FGFDMExec.cpp
+++ b/src/FGFDMExec.cpp
@@ -440,6 +440,11 @@ void FGFDMExec::LoadInputs(unsigned int idx)
     Propagate->in.J            = MassBalance->GetJ();
     Propagate->in.Jinv         = MassBalance->GetJinv();
     Propagate->in.DeltaT       = dT;
+    // FDM patch 0003: does any contact point touch? Every point counts, wheels (BOGEY) and airframe
+    // (STRUCTURE); gear/wow would only count the wheels.
+    Propagate->in.AnyContact   = false;
+    for (int i = 0; i < GroundReactions->GetNumGearUnits(); i++)
+      if (GroundReactions->GetGearUnit(i)->GetWOW()) { Propagate->in.AnyContact = true; break; }
     break;
   case eInput:
     break;
